FFmpeg's VC-1 decoder does not follow SMPTE 421M on range reduction (Main profile RANGERED / RANGEREDFRM). This patch, against FFmpeg 7.0
(libavcodec of 2024-04-23, revision 092f84b614; FFmpeg 9.0 decodes the test streams the same way), makes it follow clauses 8.1.1.4,
8.3.4.11 and 8.4.4.14; with it FFmpeg is the reference for the range reduced test streams (scripts/gen_vc1_test_streams.sh,
tests/data/vc1_main_rr_*). Streams without range reduction decode exactly as before.

What stock FFmpeg does, and what the standard says:
 - Intra blocks of a range reduced picture are doubled before they are put (vc1_block.c), so I pictures are stored in full range, while
   the inter blocks of a range reduced P or B picture are left in reduced range: such a picture is a mix of both ranges. The standard
   reconstructs the whole picture in reduced range (overlap smoothing and the in-loop filter included), keeps that reconstruction as the
   reference, and outputs a copy scaled up, Y = CLIP((Y - 128) * 2 + 128) (Cb and Cr the same).
 - A reference is scaled down, ((Y - 128) >> 1) + 128, whenever the current picture is range reduced (vc1_mc.c), whatever the
   reference's own RANGEREDFRM, and never scaled up. The standard scales it only when the two differ: down when only the current picture
   is reduced, up (with clipping) when only the reference is, before intensity compensation. A B picture has the RANGEREDFRM of its
   backward anchor, so only its forward reference can need it.

The patch: every picture remembers its RANGEREDFRM (Picture.vc1_rangeredfrm), intra blocks are no longer doubled, motion compensation
scales a reference by the difference of the two (vc1_range_adjust), and a range reduced picture is output as a scaled up copy
(vc1_output_range, through av_frame_make_writable), the reference staying as it was reconstructed.

Apply in an FFmpeg 7.0 source tree: patch -p1 < ffmpeg-vc1-range-reduction.patch

